 GEPA+Merge Figure 16: Generalization gaps for different optimization methods. Following Wan et al. (2024), we visualize the generalization gap (i.e., the difference between final test set performance and the best achieved validation performance) for different optimizers. While Wan et al. (2024) previously observed that exemplars tend to generalize better, our results suggest that instructions generated by reflective prompt evolution can achieve stronger generalization as well as improved overall performance. We hypothesize this difference may be due to the improving capabilities of the underlying LLMs, as more recent models are both better at adhering to instructions and capable of reflecting on their outputs. 2000 3000 4000 5000 6000 7000 Aggregate Prompt Token Count (Cost Proxy) 60 62 64 66 68 Aggregate Score GEPA Abl:SelectBestCandidate GEPA+Merge MIPROv2 Comparing Scores vs Prompt Size (GPT-4.1 Mini) MIPROv2 Abl:SelectBestCandidate GEPA GEPA+Merge (a) GPT-4.1 Mini. 2000 3000 4000 5000 6000 Aggregate Prompt Token Count (Cost Proxy) 55 56 57 58 59 60 61 Aggregate Score GEPA Abl:SelectBestCandidate GEPA+Merge MIPROv2 Comparing Scores vs Prompt Size (Qwen3 8B) MIPROv2 Abl:SelectBestCandidate GEPA GEPA+Merge (b) Qwen3 8B. Figure 17: These plots visualize the final aggregate scores against the aggregate prompt size (across all benchmarks) of the final optimized system for each optimizer. It can be seen that GEPA consistently produces prompts that are around less than 33% of the size of MIPROv2's prompts, while getting higher performance. Most of GEPA's prompt tokens are used for providing instructions, whereas most of MIPROv2's prompt tokens pertain to few-shot examples. Aggregate HotpotQA IFBench HoVer PUPA 2000 4000 6000 8000 10000 12000 14000 16000 Prompt Size (in tokens) 7511.8 15454.0 2637.0 5567.0 6389.0 4.3x lower 7.2x lower 2.1x lower 2.4x lower 5.3x lower 4.8x lower 7.5x lower 2.1x lower 3.3x lower 5.1x lower Optimized Prompt Size across optimizers (lower is